## 12. Engine reliability (follow-up study)

{% set vk = V.raw('config', 'engine_reliability.verdict.k') -%}
{% set kk = vk | string -%}
**Purpose:** V1 of the lightweight validation stays not passed and is never judged again. This study asked the same kind of question on a new timepoint T3 with an engine that addresses why V1 failed (a label that counted co-mention as realisation, the candidate-generation bottleneck, low power, and reuse of the verdict data for development). Labels, candidates and k differ, so the numbers are not compared with V1 (design §18, `preregistration_engine.md`). The verdict rules were fixed at the engine preregistration freeze commit {{ V.v('engine_verdict_T3', 'meta.preregistration_freeze_commit') }} before T3 was opened once.

**Label redefinition:** among triads realised significantly under the null model, only those with a paper whose title and abstract hold all three terms in a window of adjacent sentences without a comparison cue count as positive. The rule was checked against human judgements. The first attempt missed the adoption criterion (the lower bound of the combination share among rule positives above the upper bound among rule negatives); after one revision the combination share was {{ V.v('label_evidence_T1_attempt1_score', 'rule_positive.combination_share', 'd2') }} {{ V.ci('label_evidence_T1_attempt1_score', 'rule_positive.wilson_lower', 'rule_positive.wilson_upper', 'd2') }} among rule positives and {{ V.v('label_evidence_T1_attempt1_score', 'rule_negative.combination_share', 'd2') }} {{ V.ci('label_evidence_T1_attempt1_score', 'rule_negative.wilson_lower', 'rule_negative.wilson_upper', 'd2') }} among rule negatives. Nearly half of the rule positives are still not combinations, so the label stays noisy.

**Candidate generation:** a conditional generator adds a third term to pairs that co-occurred stably in the build window. On the development timepoint T2 the share of positives it can reach (its ceiling) is {{ V.v('conditional_coverage', 'timepoints.T2.label_v2.stable.coverage', 'pct1') }}, far above the recall of the lightweight-validation candidate rule, {{ V.v('conditional_coverage', 'timepoints.T2.label_v2.stable.candidate_recall', 'pct1') }}. The verdict covers one pipeline, this generator and the engine (P, H, T, R) on its candidates; the learned third-term score is a secondary analysis.

**How the verdict values were set:** the budget B and k came from a rule fixed before the power analysis on the development data (T2): the values with the highest power of passing the recall and random steps in sequence. This gives k = {{ V.v('config', 'engine_reliability.verdict.k', 'int') }} and B = {{ V.v('config', 'engine_reliability.verdict.budget_multiple_of_first_candidates', 'int') }} times the candidate count of the lightweight-validation rule. Power through the nPMI-baseline step is at most {{ V.v('derived', 'engine_power_max_through_s3', 'd3') }}; this was recorded before T3 was opened. T2 was looked at many times during development, so development numbers may be optimistic.

**T3 verdict** ({{ V.v('engine_verdict_T3', 'labels.label_v2_positive', 'int') }} positives among {{ V.v('engine_verdict_T3', 'labels.realised_significant_triads', 'int') }} significant realised triads on T3, {{ V.v('engine_verdict_T3', 'labels.pool_positive', 'int') }} positives among {{ V.v('engine_verdict_T3', 'labels.pool_candidates', 'int') }} candidates, trained on {{ V.v('engine_verdict_T3', 'meta.training_timepoints', 'join') }}). A step is tested only if every earlier step passed, and passes only if the lower bounds of both bootstraps clear its threshold.

